## Cycle summary (S522)

**Did the recorded NEXT ACTION:** the width-spectrum `D_k(x)` census first-occurrence probe at k=32 — hunting down the 189 dense admissible constellations that were unrealized at the prior reach 2²⁶.

**Built** `experiments/analytic/local_pattern_census/dk_first_occurrence.py` (one CLI script, `--selftest` with 10 groups): a constant-memory **segmented sieve** that extends the aligned-window census far past 2²⁶, the **Hardy–Littlewood aligned singular series** for the linear forms `km+oᵢ`, and a statistically-grounded **falsification monitor** (flags a missing pattern only if its HL mean count `N_S(x) ≥ 5`, i.e. `P(0 occ) = e⁻ᴺ < 0.7%` — not the naive `x* < x`).

**Result — FULL SATURATION:** pushing from 2²⁶ to **2³⁷ (x ≈ 1.4×10¹¹)** drove the deficit **189 → 0**, so **D₃₂ = A₃₂ + 1 = 3574**. k=32 now joins k=8 (14) and k=16 (107) as a confirmed instance of the law `D_k(x) → A_k + 1`. The curve reproduced every prior checkpoint *exactly* (2¹⁶=1093, 2²⁴=3213, 2²⁶=3385 — the segmented sieve is bit-equivalent to the full sieve, asserted in selftest). The last admissible pattern to appear was the densest possible (weight-9) constellation `[1,5,7,11,17,19,25,29,31]` at x ≈ 8.3×10¹⁰, on the HL schedule (`N(occ)=1.63`).

**Validation & honesty:**
- The aligned singular series is **validated to 2% at weight 6** (obs/pred = 0.980 at 2³⁰, no survivorship bias there); the obs/pred ratio rising monotonically through 1 cleanly separates the exact-vs-constellation gap (low weight) from survivorship bias (high weight).
